from common import Batch
b=Batch('HCT',41)
def q(*args,**kw): b.add(*args,**kw)
q('S','CSS performance','Content visibility','When can content-visibility: auto help a long page, and what layout detail should accompany it?','It can let the browser skip rendering work for offscreen content. Provide a suitable contain-intrinsic-size estimate to reserve space. Test scrolling and accessibility behavior with the real content.','Skipping rendering work is not the same as deleting content from the DOM. Poor size estimates can still cause scroll-position or layout changes.','Offscreen work may be avoidable.','What size is reserved before the content is rendered?','mdn:Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/content-visibility')
q('W','Tailwind','Custom dark selector','Write the Tailwind v4 custom variant that makes dark utilities activate for .dark and its descendants.','@import "tailwindcss";\n\n@custom-variant dark (&:where(.dark, .dark *));','The custom variant replaces the default dark condition.~The selector matches the marked element and descendants.~The application still needs to manage the class and persist user preference if desired.','Override the variant’s condition.','Include both the root and its descendants.','tw:dark-mode#toggling-dark-mode-manually','/* Configure class-based dark mode in Tailwind v4 CSS. */')
q('C','HTML','Native dialog','What does dialog.showModal() provide that merely adding an open attribute does not?','It opens the dialog modally in the top layer. Content outside the modal becomes inert. Still provide an accessible name, suitable initial focus and a clear close action.','The open attribute alone displays a nonmodal dialog. Visual overlays do not automatically implement the native modal interaction model.','Modal is a behavioral state.','Does the rest of the page remain interactive?','mdn:Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/dialog')
q('M','CSS animation','Reduced motion','Which media query detects a user preference to reduce nonessential motion?','@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)','This query exposes a user accessibility preference. It is not a measurement of device frame rate or network speed. Provide an appropriate reduced-motion experience.','Respect the user’s preference.','Which media feature describes motion preference?','mdn:Web/CSS/Reference/At-rules/@media/prefers-reduced-motion',wrong=['@media (animation-speed: slow)','@media (prefers-contrast: motion)','@media (max-fps: 30)'])
q('U','Stacking','Top layer escape','Follow-up to HCT-017: how can a native modal dialog avoid being trapped behind an ancestor’s stacking context?','showModal places the dialog in the browser’s top layer. That layer is above ordinary document stacking contexts. This still requires correct dialog semantics and focus behavior.','Increasing a descendant z-index only changes ordering within its context. The top layer is a separate browser-managed stacking mechanism.','Look for a browser-managed layer.','Is this still ordinary document stacking?','mdn:Web/CSS/Guides/Positioned_layout/Top_layer')
b.save('batch09a')
b=Batch('DOM',1)
q('M','Events','Target and currentTarget','In a delegated listener, what does event.currentTarget identify?','The element whose listener is currently executing.','target identifies the event’s originating target, subject to retargeting rules. currentTarget identifies the listener owner and is meaningful during handler execution.','Separate origin from listener owner.','Where was this handler registered?','mdn:Web/API/Event/currentTarget',wrong=['Always the deepest clicked element.','Always document.body.','The element that will receive the next keyboard event.'])
q('C','Events','Propagation phases','Describe the usual propagation path of a bubbling DOM event.','Capture travels down the event path toward the target. Target listeners run at the target. Bubbling then travels outward to ancestors when the event bubbles.','Not every event bubbles. stopPropagation and listener options affect delivery, so do not assume every event reaches a delegated ancestor.','There are phases around the target.','Does this event type bubble?','mdn:Web/API/Document_Object_Model/Events#event_bubbling')
q('B','DOM updates','Untrusted innerHTML','Why is this text display unsafe, and which assignment should display the input as plain text?','innerHTML parses markup from untrusted input. Use: output.textContent = userText;','The assignment treats input as HTML.~Attacker-controlled markup can create unsafe content.~textContent inserts text without HTML parsing; intentional rich HTML needs a separate sanitization policy.','Does this API parse markup?','The requirement is plain text display.','mdn:Web/API/Node/textContent#differences_from_innerhtml','output.innerHTML = userText;')
q('W','Events','One-time listener','Write attachOnce(button, callback) that invokes callback only on the first click using listener options.','function attachOnce(button, callback) {\n  button.addEventListener("click", callback, { once: true });\n}\n','Register the callback with once enabled.~The browser removes that registration after its first invocation.~A newly registered listener would be a separate registration; once is not a lifetime ban on callbacks.','Let the event system remove the listener.','Which option specifies one invocation?','mdn:Web/API/EventTarget/addEventListener#once','// button is an HTMLButtonElement.\n// Implement attachOnce(button, callback).')
q('M','DOM queries','Static NodeList','What does querySelectorAll return with respect to later matching DOM additions?','A static NodeList that does not automatically include later additions.','querySelectorAll snapshots the matching nodes. A live HTMLCollection from another API has different behavior; the collection type and originating API matter.','Check whether the collection is live.','Must you query again after inserting nodes?','mdn:Web/API/Document/querySelectorAll',wrong=['A live collection that always adds new matches.','A single mutable CSS selector string.','An array automatically synchronized with the DOM.'])
q('C','Storage','localStorage versus sessionStorage','How do localStorage and sessionStorage differ in lifetime and scope?','Both are origin-scoped key/value storage APIs. localStorage generally persists across browsing sessions. sessionStorage is additionally scoped to a top-level browsing context and lasts for its page session.','Storage availability and persistence can be limited by browser policy. Neither API is an appropriate guarantee of secure or permanent storage for sensitive data.','Compare shared origin with page session.','Should separate tabs automatically share this session storage?','mdn:Web/API/Web_Storage_API')
q('W','Events','Delegated button handler','Write delegate(list, onRemove) for dynamically added buttons with data-id inside list. Ignore matches outside list and return cleanup.','function delegate(list, onRemove) {\n  const handler = event => {\n    if (!(event.target instanceof Element)) return;\n    const button = event.target.closest("button[data-id]");\n    if (button && list.contains(button)) onRemove(button.dataset.id);\n  };\n  list.addEventListener("click", handler);\n  return () => list.removeEventListener("click", handler);\n}\n','The ancestor receives bubbling clicks.~closest finds a matching button even when an icon was clicked.~The containment check keeps matches inside the delegated region, and cleanup removes the same handler identity.','Listen on a stable ancestor.','Find the nearest matching actionable element.','mdn:Web/API/Element/closest','// list is an Element in the same document as its buttons.\n// Implement delegate(list, onRemove).')
q('B','Events','Listener identity cleanup','Why does the listener remain, and what change allows removal?','The two arrow functions have different identities. Store one function and pass that same reference to both addEventListener and removeEventListener.','Registration stores a callback identity.~The later arrow creates a different function.~Removal needs the same type, callback and matching capture setting; identical source text is insufficient.','Functions are objects with identity.','Was the same function reference reused?','mdn:Web/API/EventTarget/removeEventListener','button.addEventListener("click", () => save());\nbutton.removeEventListener("click", () => save());')
q('C','Observers','MutationObserver scope','When should MutationObserver be used instead of repeatedly polling the DOM?','Use it to observe configured DOM mutations such as child-list or attribute changes. Specify the smallest useful subtree and options. Disconnect when the observer is no longer needed.','It reports DOM mutations, not layout size or viewport intersection directly. Broad observation and mutation-heavy callbacks can create unnecessary work or feedback loops.','Choose the event source that matches the change.','Is the change DOM structure or layout geometry?','mdn:Web/API/MutationObserver')
q('W','Observers','Intersection one-shot','Write observeVisible(element, onVisible) calling onVisible once when isIntersecting becomes true, then disconnecting. Return manual cleanup.','function observeVisible(element, onVisible) {\n  const observer = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {\n    if (entries.some(entry => entry.target === element && entry.isIntersecting)) {\n      observer.disconnect();\n      onVisible();\n    }\n  });\n  observer.observe(element);\n  return () => observer.disconnect();\n}\n','Observe the element relative to the default viewport root.~Wait for an intersecting entry rather than assuming the first callback means visible.~Disconnect before the action so later notifications do not repeat it.','Observer callbacks also report nonintersection.','Which entry property represents intersection?','mdn:Web/API/IntersectionObserver','// Implement observeVisible(element, onVisible).')
q('S','Observers','ResizeObserver choice','A chart must respond to its container changing size even when the window does not resize. Which API fits?','Use ResizeObserver on the container. Recompute only the work needed for the reported size. Avoid an endless loop where the callback repeatedly changes the size it observes.','Window resize misses layout changes caused by panels, fonts or parent content. An element observer follows the actual dependency.','Observe the element’s dimensions.','Can layout change without a viewport change?','mdn:Web/API/ResizeObserver')
q('B','Fetch','HTTP error handling','Why does this catch not run for a normal HTTP 404 response, and what check is missing?','fetch usually fulfills for HTTP error statuses. Add: if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`); before parsing.','The network request successfully receives an HTTP response.~A 404 is represented by status, not automatically by promise rejection.~Check ok explicitly and handle body decoding failures separately.','Transport failure differs from HTTP failure.','Which response flag covers successful status codes?','mdn:Web/API/Fetch_API/Using_Fetch#checking_response_status','fetch("/api/user")\n  .then(response => response.json())\n  .catch(showError);')
q('M','Storage','Storage event receiver','When one same-origin tab changes localStorage, which window normally receives the storage event?','Other same-origin browsing contexts using that storage area, not the writing window.','The event supports cross-context notification. The initiating context already knows its own write and does not receive the same event. sessionStorage has narrower sharing rules.','Consider cross-tab communication.','Does the writer need to be notified of its own operation?','mdn:Web/API/Window/storage_event',wrong=['Only the writing window.','Every window on every origin.','Only a service worker.'])
q('W','History','Client navigation state','Write goToSearch(query) that pushes a same-origin /search URL with q encoded through URLSearchParams, without reloading.','function goToSearch(query) {\n  const url = new URL("/search", location.origin);\n  url.searchParams.set("q", query);\n  history.pushState({ query }, "", url);\n}\n','Build a same-origin URL.~URLSearchParams encodes the query value.~pushState adds a history entry without loading a document; the application still needs to update its UI and handle popstate navigation.','History changes do not render an app.','Use a URL API for query encoding.','mdn:Web/API/History/pushState','// Implement goToSearch(query) in a browser document.')
q('C','History','popstate behavior','Does calling history.pushState immediately fire popstate?','No, pushState does not itself dispatch popstate. History traversal can dispatch it when the active entry changes. Update the UI explicitly for programmatic pushes and handle traversal separately.','Listening only for popstate leaves programmatic pushes without automatic rendering. Changing the URL and changing application state are related but separate responsibilities.','Creation and traversal differ.','Was an entry pushed or traversed to?','mdn:Web/API/Window/popstate_event')
q('S','Storage','IndexedDB choice','Why choose IndexedDB over localStorage for a large offline dataset with indexed lookups?','IndexedDB provides asynchronous structured storage with transactions and indexes. localStorage is synchronous string storage. Design schema upgrades, quota handling and eviction expectations explicitly.','Asynchronous storage avoids blocking the main thread in the same way as synchronous reads and writes. No browser storage API should be treated as unlimited or infallible.','Consider data volume and query requirements.','Do you need transactions and indexes?','mdn:Web/API/IndexedDB_API')
q('U','Events','Passive listener contract','Follow-up to DOM-004: what does passive: true promise, and what can the listener no longer do successfully?','It promises not to cancel the event’s default action. Calling preventDefault from that listener will not cancel it. Use it where appropriate for scroll-related performance without breaking required interaction behavior.','Passive does not mean the callback is asynchronous or never runs. It changes the cancellation contract.','The browser needs to know whether scrolling can proceed.','Will the handler need to cancel the default action?','mdn:Web/API/EventTarget/addEventListener#using_passive_listeners')
q('C','CORS','Browser enforcement','What does CORS control, and why is it not server-side authorization?','It controls whether browser scripts may read certain cross-origin responses. The server declares permitted origins and related conditions in response headers. Nonbrowser callers and server permissions still need independent authentication and authorization.','Blocking script access to a response does not mean every request was prevented from reaching the server. CORS is not a universal request firewall.','Separate response access from resource permission.','Can a nonbrowser client call the endpoint?','mdn:Web/HTTP/Guides/CORS')
q('B','CORS','Credentials and wildcard','Why is this response configuration invalid for a credentialed cross-origin browser fetch?','A credentialed response cannot use Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *. Return an explicitly allowed request origin and appropriate Vary: Origin behavior.','The browser evaluates the credentials mode and CORS headers together.~The wildcard does not grant credentialed response access.~Validate origins against an allowlist rather than blindly reflecting arbitrary origins.','Credentials require an explicit origin policy.','Can a wildcard authorize cookies for every origin?','mdn:Web/HTTP/Guides/CORS#credentialed_requests_and_wildcards','Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *\nAccess-Control-Allow-Credentials: true')
q('S','Workers','Worker limitations','A worker must process a large dataset and update a table. How should responsibilities be divided?','Move CPU-heavy data processing into the worker. Send results through messages. Keep DOM updates on the main thread because dedicated workers cannot directly manipulate the document DOM.','Workers provide a separate execution context, not shared direct DOM access. Message copying or transfer costs should be included in the performance design.','Separate computation from document ownership.','Which thread owns the DOM?','mdn:Web/API/Web_Workers_API/Using_web_workers')
q('M','Workers','Transferable buffers','What happens to an ArrayBuffer after it is successfully transferred to a worker through postMessage?','The sender’s buffer is detached.','Transfer moves ownership of the underlying resource instead of copying it. The sender must not expect to keep reading that detached buffer; the receiving side gets access.','Transfer is an ownership change.','Can both sides keep the same usable buffer?','mdn:Web/API/Web_Workers_API/Transferable_objects',wrong=['It remains a second independent copy in the sender.','The worker receives only the buffer’s string representation.','It becomes a DOM node.'])
q('A','Rendering pipeline','Critical rendering path','Explain the browser work between receiving HTML/CSS and painting initial content, and identify a common avoidable blocker.','HTML builds the DOM and CSS builds style information. The browser determines layout, paints and composites the result. A blocking script or late critical stylesheet can delay progress, so prioritize essential resources and defer unrelated work.','Downloading all bytes is not the same as displaying pixels. JavaScript can also invalidate style and layout after the initial parse.','Track parse, style, layout and paint.','Which resource must complete before progress continues?','mdn:Web/Performance/Guides/Critical_rendering_path')
q('W','Events','Abortable listener group','Write attachListeners(target, onClick, onKeydown) using one AbortController to remove both listeners through a returned cleanup function.','function attachListeners(target, onClick, onKeydown) {\n  const controller = new AbortController();\n  target.addEventListener("click", onClick, { signal: controller.signal });\n  target.addEventListener("keydown", onKeydown, { signal: controller.signal });\n  return () => controller.abort();\n}\n','Create one controller for this registration group.~Pass its signal to both listeners.~Abort removes the associated registrations. A signal cannot be reset for reuse after aborting.','One signal can own multiple registrations.','What operation ends that ownership?','mdn:Web/API/EventTarget/addEventListener#add_an_abortable_listener','// Implement attachListeners(target, onClick, onKeydown).')
q('C','Shadow DOM','Event retargeting','Why can event.target outside a shadow root identify the host instead of the internal control?','Shadow DOM retargets events across encapsulation boundaries. Event composition determines whether an event crosses a shadow boundary. composedPath can expose the available propagation path, subject to closed-root encapsulation.','Ordinary DOM ancestry assumptions can fail at shadow boundaries. A delegated handler should use the component’s public event contract rather than depend on private internals.','Encapsulation affects event exposure.','Is the event composed across the boundary?','mdn:Web/API/Event/composed')
q('U','Observers','Resize feedback loop','Follow-up to DOM-011: how can a ResizeObserver callback create a resize loop, and how should it be controlled?','Changing the observed dimensions can trigger another notification. Update only when the intended size actually differs and avoid cyclic layout dependencies. If deferring a write to a frame, still fix the underlying feedback logic.','Scheduling alone does not make a divergent size rule converge. Observer loop warnings indicate that the callback and layout should be inspected together.','Look for write-observe-write cycles.','Does each callback force another different size?','mdn:Web/API/ResizeObserver#observation_errors')
