b=Batch('ASYNC',1)
q('M','Promises','States and settlement','Which set lists the three Promise states?','pending, fulfilled, rejected','A promise starts pending and can settle once as fulfilled or rejected. Resolved is a broader concept because a promise can adopt another still-pending promise.','Settlement has two outcomes.','Is resolved necessarily already fulfilled?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise#description',wrong=['waiting, running, complete','pending, resolved, canceled','created, scheduled, destroyed'])
q('P','Promises','Executor timing','What is the exact log order?','start\nexecutor\nend\nthen','The Promise executor runs synchronously during construction.~The then callback is queued as a microtask.~The current script finishes before that reaction executes.','Construction and reactions have different timing.','Which code runs before the stack empties?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/Promise','console.log("start");\nconst promise = new Promise(resolve => {\n  console.log("executor");\n  resolve();\n});\npromise.then(() => console.log("then"));\nconsole.log("end");')
q('P','Promises','Chaining returned values','What does the final handler log?','6','The first reaction receives 2 and returns 3.~That return fulfills the next promise with 3.~The following reaction doubles it. Forgetting a return would pass undefined instead.','Each then creates a new promise.','What value fulfills the next link?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/then','Promise.resolve(2)\n  .then(value => value + 1)\n  .then(value => console.log(value * 2));')
q('B','Promises','Floating asynchronous work','Why does the next then run without waiting for save, and which line fixes the chain?','The handler does not return save’s promise. Use: .then(data => save(data));','The first handler starts save but returns undefined.~Its resulting promise fulfills immediately with undefined.~Returning save’s promise makes the next link adopt its completion and rejection.','Chaining follows returned work.','Which promise does the handler expose?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Guide/Using_promises#floating_promises','load()\n  .then(data => { save(data); })\n  .then(showSuccess);')
q('P','async await','Await continuation','What does this script log?','inside\noutside\nafter','Calling run executes synchronously until await.~Even awaiting a nonpromise value suspends the continuation.~The outside log runs before the resumed async function.','Await creates a continuation.','Does awaiting 1 still yield?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/await#control_flow_effects_of_await','async function run() {\n  console.log("inside");\n  await 1;\n  console.log("after");\n}\nrun();\nconsole.log("outside");')
q('C','async await','Rejection handling','How does an awaited rejected promise interact with try/catch?','await throws the rejection reason at the await expression. A surrounding try/catch can handle it. Without handling, the async function’s returned promise rejects.','An unawaited promise is not caught merely because it was created inside try. Error handling must cover the asynchronous completion being observed.','Await turns rejection into a throw at that point.','Is the promise actually awaited inside try?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/async_function')
q('P','Combinators','Promise.all order','What order do these results retain?','first,second','Promise.all consumes its inputs in order.~Its result array preserves that input order, regardless of completion order.~Both inputs already fulfill here, so no timing assumptions are needed.','Result position follows the iterable.','Is completion order used for indexing?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/all','Promise.all([Promise.resolve("first"), Promise.resolve("second")])\n  .then(values => console.log(values.join(",")));')
q('P','Combinators','allSettled records','What exact string is logged?','fulfilled,rejected','allSettled waits for every input to settle.~It produces one status record per input in order.~An input rejection becomes a rejected record rather than rejecting the combined promise.','Failures become data records.','Which field identifies each outcome?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/allSettled','Promise.allSettled([Promise.resolve(1), Promise.reject("bad")])\n  .then(results => console.log(results.map(result => result.status).join(",")));')
q('M','Combinators','Promise.any purpose','Which combinator fulfills with the first fulfillment while ignoring earlier rejections unless every input rejects?','Promise.any','any waits for a successful input. If every input rejects, it rejects with AggregateError; race instead accepts the first settlement of either kind.','Choose the first success, not the first settlement.','What happens if one input rejects early?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/any',wrong=['Promise.race','Promise.all','Promise.allSettled'])
q('P','Combinators','race first settlement','What is logged by this race of already-settled inputs?','rejected:bad','Handlers are registered in iterable order.~The already-rejected first input schedules its reaction first.~The race rejects with bad and later fulfillment cannot change that settlement.','There is no network timing race here.','Which queued reaction settles the race first?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/race','Promise.race([Promise.reject("bad"), Promise.resolve("ok")])\n  .then(console.log, reason => console.log(`rejected:${reason}`));')
q('T','Event loop','Tasks and microtasks','In a browser or Node script, what is logged?','script\npromise\nmicrotask\ntimer','The synchronous log runs first.~Promise reactions and queueMicrotask callbacks share FIFO microtask ordering here.~The timer task runs after the microtask checkpoint. This does not compare competing task sources.','Drain microtasks before the timer task.','Which microtask was queued first?','mdn:Web/API/HTML_DOM_API/Microtask_guide','setTimeout(() => console.log("timer"), 0);\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log("promise"));\nqueueMicrotask(() => console.log("microtask"));\nconsole.log("script");')
q('T','Event loop','Nested microtask ordering','What does this queue sequence log?','a\nb\nc','The queue initially contains callbacks a and b.~Callback a appends c to the end.~The checkpoint continues through b and then c before moving to another task.','New microtasks join the queue tail.','What is already queued when c is added?','mdn:Web/API/HTML_DOM_API/Microtask_guide#microtasks','queueMicrotask(() => {\n  console.log("a");\n  queueMicrotask(() => console.log("c"));\n});\nqueueMicrotask(() => console.log("b"));')
q('S','Animation','requestAnimationFrame timing','Why use requestAnimationFrame for visual updates instead of assuming setTimeout(fn, 16) matches the display?','requestAnimationFrame schedules work before a rendering opportunity. Its timestamp supports frame-rate-independent motion. Display rates vary and background tabs can pause or throttle callbacks.','A fixed timer interval is not synchronized to painting. Do not assume every callback represents exactly 16 milliseconds.','The browser controls rendering opportunities.','Can displays refresh at different rates?','mdn:Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame')
q('W','Cancellation','Abortable fetch wrapper','Write loadJSON(url, signal) that passes cancellation to fetch, checks HTTP success and returns parsed JSON.','async function loadJSON(url, signal) {\n  const response = await fetch(url, { signal });\n  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\n  return response.json();\n}\n','Pass the signal to the actual fetch operation.~Check status before decoding.~Abort and parse failures propagate to the caller. Aborting client work does not undo a server-side mutation already performed.','Cancellation must reach the operation.','Which fetch option carries the signal?','mdn:Web/API/Fetch_API/Using_Fetch#canceling_a_request','// Implement loadJSON(url, signal).')
q('P','Promises','Catch recovery','What does this recovered chain log?','4','The rejection skips fulfillment handlers until catch.~catch returns 3, converting the chain to fulfillment.~The next then adds one. Rethrowing would keep it rejected instead.','A catch handler can return a replacement value.','What state follows a normal return?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/catch','Promise.reject(new Error("offline"))\n  .catch(() => 3)\n  .then(value => console.log(value + 1));')
q('P','Promises','Finally preserves value','What does this finally chain log?','cleanup\n7','finally runs its callback when the promise settles.~A normal return from finally does not replace the existing fulfillment value.~The next handler receives 7; throwing or returning a rejecting promise would change the outcome.','Cleanup normally preserves the outcome.','Is finally equivalent to then for return values?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/finally','Promise.resolve(7)\n  .finally(() => {\n    console.log("cleanup");\n    return 99;\n  })\n  .then(value => console.log(value));')
q('U','Combinators','Failure does not cancel siblings','Follow-up to ASYNC-007: does Promise.all automatically cancel remaining fetches when one input rejects?','No, it rejects its combined result without canceling the underlying operations. Use an explicit cancellation mechanism if appropriate. Ensure every operation’s cleanup and failure handling match the application’s policy.','Promises represent completion and do not generally own cancellation. AbortController affects operations that actually observe its signal.','Aggregation and cancellation are separate.','Who owns the underlying requests?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise/all#promiseall_fail-fast_behavior')
q('T','Async iteration','Sequential consumption','What does this async generator consumer log?','1\n2\ndone','The generator yields 1 and then an awaited 2.~for await waits for each next result before executing the body.~After completion, the final log runs. This is sequential consumption, not parallel processing.','Each iteration awaits a result.','When can the loop reach the final statement?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/for-await...of','async function* values() {\n  yield 1;\n  yield Promise.resolve(2);\n}\n(async () => {\n  for await (const value of values()) console.log(value);\n  console.log("done");\n})();')
q('B','Async iteration','Async forEach bug','Why does await not wait for these saves, and what sequential loop replaces the statement?','forEach returns undefined and ignores callback promises. Use: for (const item of items) { await save(item); }','forEach starts callbacks without collecting their promises.~await receives undefined rather than their completion.~A for-of loop awaits each save sequentially; Promise.all with map would be the parallel alternative.','Check the method’s return value.','Where are the callback promises collected?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/forEach#description','await items.forEach(async item => {\n  await save(item);\n});\nshowSuccess();')
q('A','Event loop','Microtask starvation','Why can recursively scheduling queueMicrotask prevent a page from responding or painting?','A microtask checkpoint drains the queue until it is empty. Continuously adding more microtasks can prevent it from finishing. Split long work across tasks or use a worker when appropriate.','Microtasks are not a general-purpose yielding mechanism for expensive work. Awaiting an already-fulfilled promise repeatedly can have similar starvation behavior.','The queue must become empty.','Does this scheduling choice let the browser reach rendering?','mdn:Web/API/HTML_DOM_API/Microtask_guide#warning')
b.save('batch09c')
